nny lines  \n\nIf a line is vertical, horizontal or has slope \\(-1\\) then its intersection with the triangle\n\\(\\{a>0,b>0,a+b\\le n+1\\}\\) is a line segment of length at most \\(n\\); the three extremal lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\qquad y=1,\\qquad x+y=n+1\n\\]\n\neach contain exactly \\(n\\) points of \\(S_n\\). Hence  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{|L\\cap S_n|\\le n}\n\\qquad\\text{for every non‑sunny line }L.\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nAssume we have \\(n\\) distinct lines covering all points of \\(S_n\\) and exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny.  \nUsing (3) for the sunny lines and (4) for the remaining \\(n-k\\) lines we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\Bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\Bigr\\rfloor+(n-k)n\\ge |S_n|\n=\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\tag{5}\n\\]\n\nSince \\(\\bigl\\lfloor\\frac{n+1}{3}\\bigr\\rfloor\\le\\frac{n+1}{3}\\), inequality (5) implies  \n\n\\[\nk\\frac{n+1}{3}+n^2-kn\\ge\\frac{n(n+1)}2 .\n\\]\n\nRearranging gives  \n\n\\[\nk\\bigl(n-3\\bigr)\\le n(n-1).\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(n\\ge3\\), the factor \\(n-3\\) is non‑negative, and we obtain  \n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1 .\n\\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThus **no configuration can contain \\(n\\) sunny lines**; the maximal possible number of sunny lines is \\(n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 4.  Construction attaining every \\(k\\le n-1\\)\n\n#### 4.1 Base covering\nFor each integer \\(x\\) with \\(1\\le x\\le n\\) let  \n\n\\[\nV_x:\\;x=\\text{const}=x .\n\\]\n\nThe family \\(\\mathcal V=\\{V_x\\}_{x=1}^{n}\\) consists of \\(n\\) distinct vertical (hence non‑sunny) lines and clearly covers every point of \\(S_n\\).\n\n#### 4.2 Sunny replacement\nFor any integer \\(c\\) with \\(1\\le c\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_c:\\;\\text{the line through }P_c=(c,1)\\text{ and }Q_c=(c+1,n-c).\n\\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(L_c\\) is  \n\n\\[\nm_c=\\frac{n-c-1}{1}=n-c-1\\ge 1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(L_c\\) is sunny (